      "title": {
        "en": "A mirror on the Moon"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Apollo astronauts left panels of retroreflectors on the lunar surface. A retroreflector sends light straight back the way it came, whatever angle it arrives from."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "aim": 0.9,
        "dist": 384400
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Aim and fire"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "Line the laser up on the reflector and fire a pulse. Light travels at about 300 000 km/s, yet the trip there and back still takes about two and a half seconds."
      },
      "equation": null,
      "modelState": {
        "aim": 0,
        "dist": 384400
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Time gives distance"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "The pulse covers the distance twice, out and back. Halve the round-trip time and multiply by the speed of light to get the distance to the Moon."
      },
      "equation": "d = \\dfrac{c\\,t}{2}",
      "modelState": {
        "aim": 0,
        "dist": 384400
      }
    },
    {
      "title": {
        "en": "Only a few photons come home"
      },
      "text": {
        "en": "The beam spreads to kilometres wide by the time it arrives, and the reflector is small. Of a pulse of about 10¹⁷ photons, only a handful return to the telescope. Change the distance to see the time change as the Moon's orbit carries it nearer and further."
